    "note": "Kenney ship-ocean-liner (GLB with the kit colormap), same kit and fetch pin as boat-fishing. A four-funnel Titanic-style liner, stylized and stubby: the toy is only 2.4 times as long as it is tall, so no single scale is real. Height 70 m keel to mast tops (the funnel tops sit at about 62 m; a modern cruise ship stands about 70 m) gives a 167 m hull and a 37 m beam: short for a liner (real ones run 200 to 300 m) but a believable length for a smaller ocean ship, and beam and height read right at the far/mid distances films place it. Bow toward +Z (image-left in the lab left view; the funnels rake aft). y = 0 is the keel; the waterline sits at about y = 11.5 m (the top of the red boot-topping, where the dark hull band starts), so a film sinks the ship 11.5 m into its water plane. Cream superstructure, dark-brown hull band, funnel tops, masts and rigging, buff funnels (White Star style), red boot-topping and flags. Static, no clips."
  },
